A cache keeps the recursive style but removes the repeated calculations. When a value has already been found, the function returns it from the dictionary instead of opening the same recursion tree again.

The base cases are stored too, so `n = 0` and `n = 1` are handled safely. This uses more memory than a two-variable loop, but the code is still close to the Fibonacci formula.

```python
class Solution:
    def fib(self, n):
        cache = {0: 0, 1: 1}

        def find_value(value):
            if value not in cache:
                cache[value] = find_value(value - 1) + find_value(value - 2)
            return cache[value]

        return find_value(n)
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because each value from `0` through `n` is computed once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the cache and recursion calls.

Only the last two Fibonacci values are needed to create the next one, so there is no reason to keep the whole sequence. The variables `previous` and `current` slide forward after every addition.

For `n = 6`, the updates finish with `8`. The small inputs `0` and `1` are returned before the loop, which also avoids special handling inside it.

```java
class Solution {
    public int fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return n;
        }

        int previous = 0;
        int current = 1;

        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            int next = previous + current;
            previous = current;
            current = next;
        }

        return current;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the loop visits each index once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, with only a fixed number of variables.

Fast doubling calculates two Fibonacci values together. For a value `k`, the formulas produce `F(2k)` and `F(2k + 1)` from `F(k)` and `F(k + 1)`. The binary form of `n` then lets the recursion skip large ranges.

The helper returns a pair, which keeps both needed values available. The input is not changed. For this problem's usual small integer range, `int` is enough.

```go
func fib(n int) int {
    first, _ := pair(n)
    return first
}

func pair(n int) (int, int) {
    if n == 0 {
        return 0, 1
    }

    a, b := pair(n / 2)
    c := a * (2*b - a)
    d := a*a + b*b

    if n%2 == 0 {
        return c, d
    }
    return d, c + d
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`, since `n` is halved on each recursive call.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursion stack.

This version builds an array from the start of the sequence. `values[i]` stores the answer for index `i`, so the next entry can use `values[i - 1]` and `values[i - 2]`.

A quick dry run for `n = 5` is `0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5`, and the last array entry is returned. The full table is easy to inspect, although it needs linear extra space.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return n;
        }

        vector<int> values(n + 1, 0);
        values[1] = 1;

        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            values[i] = values[i - 1] + values[i - 2];
        }

        return values[n];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the table.

The recursive definition is short and matches the sequence directly. Values at `0` and `1` are returned as they are. Every larger value comes from the two previous calls.

For example, `fib(4)` becomes `fib(3) + fib(2)`, which finally gives `2`. The downside is repeated work, since the same smaller values are calculated many times.

```java
class Solution {
    public int fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return n;
        }
        return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(2^n)` because the calls branch repeatedly.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the deepest recursion stack.

